From dd8236716669bf8a7adcff89fd288a7ef2edacb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 10:16:06 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_tasks: one-line task labels, and a Rename the panel
 keeps

A task's label could span several lines. Both rows built it with
`Label::new(description).truncate()`, and `truncate()` only stops soft
wrapping: a hard `\n` -- a Bash heredoc, say -- still starts a new line
inside a fixed-height row, so 0037's card and 0035's panel drew the extra
lines over the rows below them (seen live in zeo -r7). Every 013/014
fixture was a one-line "Task {id}", which is why no test saw it. Story 022.

What a row shows now, in the card and in the panel:

  * one line: every run of whitespace, line breaks and tabs included, is
    collapsed to one space (`agent_ui::collapse_label`), then rendered
    `.single_line().truncate()`;
  * at most 60 characters (`TASK_LABEL_MAX_CHARS`): a longer label is cut
    to 59 plus an ellipsis (`display_label`); exactly 60 stays uncut --
    `util::truncate_and_trailoff(s, n)` cuts whenever the byte length passes
    n, so it is only called once the character count is over 60;
  * the full collapsed text in the row's tooltip, and the shown text in
    every screen-reader label of the row.

Rename. Every panel row -- running, ended, interrupted or archived -- has a
Rename action (pencil, "Rename This Task", keyboard-reachable). It swaps the
label for a one-line editor holding the current label, selected. Enter or
leaving the editor saves; Escape cancels; a blank name restores the agent's
description; what is kept is cut to 60 characters. Escape is handled as
`editor::actions::Cancel` as well as `menu::Cancel`: inside an Editor the
default keymap dispatches the former, so the latter alone leaves Escape
dead. The editor is rendered outside the row's ButtonLike, so a click in
it never opens the thread, and rows are full width so its slot is not laid
out at 0px by uniform_list.
An editor that vanishes without an answer -- its row scrolled out of
uniform_list's range, hidden by a filter, or gone -- discards the edit
instead of saving half a label. Asking what holds focus cannot tell that
apart from a real blur: the Workspace refocuses the panel before Blurred
arrives. The panel's own on_focus_lost fires first, and that is the flag.

Where the label lives. The panel's history gains migration step 2,
`ALTER TABLE agent_task_history ADD COLUMN label TEXT` -- appended, step 1
untouched, since sqlez migrations are positional; a 0035 database opens
with every row intact and label NULL. `TaskHistoryStore::rename` updates
memory, then the `agent_ui::TaskLabels` global, then queues the write, so a
failed write still shows the label this session and is logged with the
session and task ids. The global is how the card shows the user's label:
agent_tasks_panel depends on agent_ui, never the reverse. Loading the
history refills it, so a label survives a restart; later updates of the
task never clear it.

Dock. The panel now docks at the bottom by default (320px tall) instead of
the left: the agent panel, which holds 0037's card, defaults to the left
dock, so the two hid each other. A dock position the user saved is kept.

Paired with claude-agent-acp-plus 0.25.0, which holds a foreground shell
task back for 5 s and labels shell tasks with the tool call's own
description; with an older adapter everything above still holds, the
lists are only fuller.

Tests: agent_ui task_label 16, tasks_card 96; agent_tasks_panel 97 (new:
history_db/label, store/rename, panel/list_label, actions_rename,
label_contract, rename_vanish, dock_default). GPUI test windows build no accessibility tree, so each
crate records the aria labels it renders in a #[cfg(test)] thread-local.
Live proof on a built Zeo p20261004: a 10 s heredoc listed on one line under
its description, a 79-character rename stored as 60, kept across a restart.

(0.2.3_p20261009): a duplicate test of the dock fallback is removed from persistence_tests
-- dock_default_tests already covers it. (Here because this patch is the last to
touch that file.)
